 can be formed therefore the term ranking  scale  is often used in this case however the  distances  between the values are not interpretable  so it is not possible to make a  statement  about the absolute distance between two  values  therefore the characteristics are it is  the next higher level of measurement  it is possible to determine equal and  unequal categories  and greater or smaller there is a  logical hierarchy of categories  the distances between the numerical  values are not equal  therefore they cannot be interpreted for  example  ordinary variables can be frequency of  television  or the question the government is doing  a good job in the first case frequency  of television the groups are  daily several times a week less  frequently  or never so you can distinguish between  these  answers and it is possible to rank them  the same we will have for this question  the government is doing a good job  agree with undecided or declines  therefore compared to the nominal scale  we have also  categories which can be distinguished  but further it is possible  to rank them now let's have a closer  look at the last level of measurement  matrix variables have the highest  possible level of measurement  with a matrix level of measurement the  characteristic values  can be compared and sorted and distances  between the values can be calculated  so the characteristics are it is the  highest level of measurement  it is possible to create rankings equal  and unequal  is possible and for sure it is possible  to define which value is greater  and smaller and differences in sums can  be formed meaningfully  as an example for matrix variables we  have the income  the weight the age and the electricity  consumption  the matrix scale level can be further  subdivided into interval scale  and ratio scale to understand the  difference  let's look at these the example for the  ratio scale  the time of marathon runners is measured  here the statement can be made  that the fastes